Q: Warnings about deleted constructors/operators/assignment operators A: <p>These are Level 4 diagnostics triggered by <code>/W4</code>. <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/error-messages/compiler-warnings/compiler-warning-level-4-c4623?view=msvc-170" rel="nofollow noreferrer">C4623</a> and <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/error-messages/compiler-warnings/compiler-warning-level-4-c4626?view=msvc-170" rel="nofollow noreferrer">C4626</a> just mean the compiler couldn't generate a default constructor or assignment operator because your <code>pretty_printed_container</code> has a reference member and a const member.</p>
<p>Since you aren't actually using those functions, the warnings are harmless. They'd only turn into errors if you tried to call them. But I'd just explicitly delete them to keep the output clean:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template &lt;typename T&gt;
struct pretty_printed_container {
    using value_type = T;
    const T&amp; container;

    pretty_printed_container() = delete;
    pretty_printed_container(const pretty_printed_container&amp;) = delete;
    pretty_printed_container&amp; operator=(const pretty_printed_container&amp;) = delete;
    pretty_printed_container&amp; operator=(pretty_printed_container&amp;&amp;) = delete;
};
</code></pre>
<p>You should probably do the same for the <code>initializer_list</code> specialization if it's throwing the same warnings.</p>

Q: excel4node lib to autosize excel cell in order to fit longer text A: <p>you can add alignment settings for cell setting :</p>
<pre><code>alignment: { 
    shrinkToFit: true, 
    wrapText: true
}
</code></pre>

Q: Race conditions &quot;check-then-act&quot; and &quot;read-modify-write&quot; A: <p>What is a race condition?
Check this stack-overflow <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/34510/what-is-a-race-condition">question</a>.</p>
<p>There are primarily two scenarios for race-condition: read-modify-write and check-then-act.</p>
<p>For read-modify-write classical example is of <code>counter++</code> which is not an atomic operation so leads to race condition.</p>
<p>For check-then-act there are multiple examples. One example is when you check for key existence in HashMap and then do some work in if-case. Another example is singleton class code:</p>
<pre><code>public Singleton getInstance()
{
   if(_instance == null)
   { 
      _instance = new Singleton();
   }
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can read more about them on internet. One good book on concurrency is Java Concurrency in Practice by Brian Goetz.  You can also find this <a href="http://dig.cs.illinois.edu/papers/checkThenAct.pdf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">article</a> helpful.</p>

Q: How to go from a std::vector or std::deque to a std::initiailzer_list? A: <p><strong>The length of a init-list is determined at compile time.</strong> This can't be bypassed since it's an API constraint of the boost function.</p>
<p>Another problem is that an init-list is just a view <strong>over a hidden array</strong>, not even over the objects that were passed to the constructor to create the init-list (so it doesn't matter if those objects were thin views themselves or what their lifetime was). That means you can't construct a <code>std::initializer_list</code> object in a function and return it from that function - the lifetime of its hidden backing array doesn't extend past the return. For example this is invalid:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>auto create_args(const std::string_view &amp;a, const std::string_view &amp;b) {
    return std::initializer_list{a, b};
}
</code></pre>
<p>It is, however, possible to use template metaprogramming to write a wrapper call that constructs the init-list in place. This example works for any container with <code>operator[]</code> but, as mentioned to be unavoidable above, requires the number of args to be a compile-time constant explicitly passed to the call.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;deque&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;

void f(std::initializer_list&lt;std::string_view&gt; args);

template &lt;class C, std::size_t... I&gt;
auto call_f_internal(const C &amp;args, std::index_sequence&lt;I...&gt;) {
    return f({std::string_view{args[I]}...});
}

template &lt;std::size_t N, class C&gt;
auto f(const C &amp;args) {
    return call_f_internal(args, std::make_index_sequence&lt;N&gt;{});
}

int main() {
    std::deque&lt;std::string&gt; args;
    args.emplace_back(&quot;ab&quot;);
    args.emplace_back(&quot;bcd&quot;);

    f&lt;2&gt;(args);
}
</code></pre>
<p>The best solution, of course, is an API that doesn't require an initializer list unless it's truly the intent to have the caller pass explicit lists of arguments at the point of call. If you're really at Boost v2, the <a href="https://www.boost.org/doc/libs/latest/libs/process/doc/html/index.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">documentation</a> offers an overload that doesn't require an init-list:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>  // Construct a child from a property list and launch it using the default launcher..
  template&lt;typename Args, typename ... Inits&gt;
  explicit basic_process(
      executor_type executor,
      const filesystem::path&amp; exe,
      Args&amp;&amp; args, Inits&amp;&amp;... inits);
</code></pre>
<p>Other than the signature, this type of overloads is <em>woefully</em> undocumented, but it accepts a variable of type <code>std::deque&lt;std::string&gt;</code> and similar.</p>
